# `#519` sgirandomcolour -- THE JOINT-CORNER CLOSURE: is ONE row enough, and what is the class boundary?

Nonce: `CODEX_519_CORNER_20260910`. Open with `VERDICT:` naming the ROW SET you would ship, then echo the
nonce.

Ordinary regression-test design on a graphics-engine helper in an emulator written in C99. **Please do not
simply ratify** -- and in particular do not ratify the brief's own arithmetic, which is the thing most
likely to be wrong here. **The tree is `C:\DocumentNoSnc\CC\GXEMUL\GXEMUL-SEC\`**; three unrelated
2021-vintage copies sit elsewhere under the project root, so line numbers off by hundreds mean you are in
one of those. **Enumerate arithmetic, do not reason about it.** **Verdict line first.**

## §C0 -- state

HEAD `4eac0b1`. `src/devices/dev_sgi_re.c` carries the `#519` fix UNCOMMITTED and the round is HELD. The
kill tables and instruments are committed under `regress/artifacts/sgirandomcolour/`.

## §C -- SETTLED PREMISES (check them; each is one grep away)

S1. **THE DEFECT.** In `horrible_getputpixel()` (grep anchor `void horrible_getputpixel`), the PUT-side
    `switch (color_mode)` had a `default:` arm that filled `buf[0..3]` with `random()` and called an
    UNLATCHED per-pixel `fatal()`. The common store BELOW the switch then wrote those bytes to guest
    memory. **Twelve of the sixteen type nibbles reach that arm, and `DE_MODE_TYPE_YCRCB` -- a
    DOCUMENTED mode -- is one of them.** Measured at rung 3: a destination pre-painted `0x11223344` read
    back `0x56e8857a` and `0x38abc4e1` on two runs, different each run because `main.c` seeds `random()`
    per process. Severity HIGH: a guest gets four host-PRNG bytes written into its own memory.
S2. **THE FIX** is option-set member 3, DECLINE + LATCHED ANNOUNCE: `int ok = 1;` before the switch, the
    `default:` arm sets `ok = 0` and announces once on its own new latch bit `SGIRE_W_BADCOLOR = 1 << 12`,
    and the store below became `if (ok)`. **A declined PUT stores nothing, so the destination keeps what
    the guest had** -- deliberately not a zero fill, which would publish a colour the guest never asked
    for.
S3. **THE FIELD DERIVATION**, verbatim from the function:
        `uint32_t color_mode = mode & DE_MODE_TYPE_MASK;`   (`DE_MODE_TYPE_MASK` is `0x000000f0`)
        `int bufdepth = 1 << ((mode >> 8) & 3);`            (so bufdepth is in BYTES: 1, 2, 4 or 8)
        `uint32_t tlb_mode = (mode >> 10) & 0x7;`
        `int linear = tlb_mode > 3;`                        (TLB_A/B/C are tiled; LIN_A/LIN_B are linear)
S4. **THE TWO ESCAPES, MEASURED.** Two clause-(d) mutants scored a CLEAN SWEEP on BOTH instruments --
    14/14 on the 14-row and 19/19 on the 19-row:
        `M13_widthdecline`   an added `if (bufdepth == 4) ok = 0;`
        `M14_lineardecline`  an added `if (linear) ok = 0;`
    They are ACCEPT-SIDE breaches: they make the helper decline writes it should accept. They escape
    because **every existing decline row drives a mode that already satisfies the added condition**, so
    the decline still happens and the row stays green.
S5. **MY OWN FIRST CLOSURE PLAN WAS THE TRAP, AND I WANT YOU TO CHECK THAT IT REALLY IS.** I proposed TWO
    rows, one at each edge -- (bufdepth 4, tiled) and (bufdepth 1, linear). I now believe the single
    mutant `if (bufdepth == 4 || linear) ok = 0;` defeats both, because the disjunction fires at each
    edge, the decline still happens, and both rows stay green.
S6. **THE PROPOSED MINIMUM IS ONE ROW AT THE JOINT CORNER: `mode = 0x00f0`.** Enumerated rather than
    reasoned:
        `0x00f0 & 0xf0`      = `0xf0` = `DE_MODE_TYPE_YCRCB` exactly -- the documented reachable type
        `(0x00f0 >> 8) & 3`  = 0     -> bufdepth = 1   (i.e. `DE_MODE_BUFDEPTH_8`)
        `(0x00f0 >> 10) & 7` = 0     -> tlb_mode 0 = TLB_A, so `linear` is FALSE
    At that cell NEITHER `bufdepth == 4` NOR `linear` holds, so all three mutants above fail to decline,
    the random-fill store lands, and the row reds. **One row, three kills** -- and it is simultaneously
    the most realistic guest input in the whole set.

## THE QUESTIONS

1. **PREMISES AS QUESTIONS -- re-derive S3 and S6 from the source, not from this brief.** Is `bufdepth`
   really in bytes? Is `0x00f0` really YCRCB AND depth-1 AND tiled at the same time, or does some other
   field in `mode` collide with the type nibble? **And check S5 by construction: does
   `if (bufdepth == 4 || linear) ok = 0;` really escape both edge rows?** If any of these is wrong the
   whole closure changes, and a predicted escape that is secretly already killed is exactly what this
   project has been burned by.
2. **IS ONE ROW THE RIGHT CLOSURE, OR DOES THE JOINT-CELLS RULE DEMAND MORE?** This project's rule is
   that DEPENDS-ON enumerates joint cells over the guard's operands and the body-read lvalues **exactly
   when the guarded body's own code distinguishes them**, taking the CROSS-PRODUCT of the partitions the
   body's own tests induce. So: read the body and enumerate what it actually tests. `bufdepth` appears in
   `tilewidth_in_pixels = 512 / bufdepth` and in `xofs`; `linear` gates an early bounds `return` and the
   address computation. **Give me the cross-product, say which cells the existing rows already hold, and
   name the SMALLEST set that closes the class.** If one row genuinely closes it, say so plainly and say
   why the other cells are clause-(e) debt rather than floor.
3. **THE `linear` EARLY RETURN IS THE TRAP I MOST SUSPECT.** `if (!linear && (x < 0 || y < 0 || x >= 2048
   || y >= 2048)) return;` -- a TILED row is subject to a bounds check a LINEAR row is not. Does that
   make any proposed corner row unreachable, or change which coordinates it must use? **A row that never
   reaches the switch is worse than no row.**
4. **THE FOURTH-PASS TEST, applied to your own answer.** After your row set ships, what is the next
   same-class mutant that escapes? If your honest answer is "none within the enumerated class", state the
   class BOUNDARY -- which edits are outside it and therefore clause-(e) debt to be disclosed in the
   contract rather than rowed. **The round needs a boundary it can defend, not a promise of more rows.**
5. **THE ORACLE.** The decline is observed by reading the destination back and finding the guest's own
   pre-painted value intact. That oracle has a known weakness: **a mutant that declines when it should
   accept also leaves the destination intact**, so accept-side and decline-side rows read the same
   channel in opposite directions. Is that sound, or does the corner row need a different channel --
   the latched announce's own text, a count, or something else? Note the announce is LATCHED ONCE per
   device, so it cannot carry more than one row.
6. **COST.** How many new sessions does your set add, and does any new row perturb an existing row's
   score? This project has measured that added rows change other mutants' scores and force a full
   re-table, so a set that doubles the run is a real cost to weigh.
7. If, while checking, you found something else -- say so.

Read-only: no builds, no probe runs, no edits, no patches. A row table plus a short justification is what
