57:
58:WHY M13 AND M14 ESCAPE -- BY READING, NOT BY RE-AIMING
59:------------------------------------------------------
60:Both are the clause-(d) operand-swap form.  The guarded body is ONE statement,
61:
62:    cpu->memory_rw(cpu, cpu->mem, tileptr + ofs, buf, bufdepth, MEM_WRITE, NO_EXCEPTIONS | PHYSICAL);
63:
64:and its read-set is { cpu, cpu->mem, tileptr, ofs, buf, bufdepth }.  M13 conditions the repair on
65:bufdepth -- the store's own LENGTH argument.  M14 conditions it on `linear', which is what selects
66:between the tileptr the tiled arms compute and the one the LIN_A arm computes.  Neither is a remote
67:lvalue: each is an operand of the very call that `if (ok)' guards.  So both fall out of the
68:DEPENDS-ON clause-(2) enumeration mechanically, the way #462's M11 fell out of `data_len'.
69:
70:THE ROWS I EXPECTED TO REDDEN were, in each case, the four decline-observing rows -- `ycrcb put',
71:`reserved put', `two declined', `indep paint' -- plus `determinism'.  None of them CAN:
72:
73:  * WIDTH.  bufdepth = 1 << ((mode >> 8) & 3).  Every row that drives a DECLINED type uses
74:    MODE_YCRCB = 0x12f0 or MODE_RESERVED = 0x1250, both carrying DE_MODE_BUFDEPTH_32 (0x200), so
75:    (mode >> 8) & 3 == 2 and bufdepth == 4 in every one of them.  The cell that M13 exempts --
76:    {declined type} x {bufdepth != 4} -- is entered by no row, so `ok = 0' still fires everywhere
77:    the instrument looks.
78:      The sharp part: the probe DOES drive BUFDEPTH_8 -- `ci8 put' uses MODE_CI8 = 0x1000 -- but
79:      only at a SUPPORTED type, where `ok' is never cleared anyway.  The width axis is exercised and
80:      the declined-type axis is exercised; their JOINT cell is not.  That is the #499 width-axis
81:      shape recurring, and it is invisible to a per-axis reading of the row list.
82:
83:  * ADDRESSING.  Every row drives LIN_A (tlb_mode 4, so linear == 1) except `latch indep' /
84:    `indep paint', which drive LIN_B (tlb_mode 5) -- and LIN_B returns from the tlb switch's OWN
85:    default arm (SGIRE_W_BADMODE) long before the colour switch, so it contributes no observation
86:    of the colour decline's store at all.  The cell {declined type} x {tiled, tlb_mode 0-2} is
87:    entered by no row.
88:
89:REACHABILITY -- both cells are guest-reachable through committed dispatch, established by reading:
90:
91:  * M13: the guest writes `mode' to DE_MODE_DST.  crmfbreg.h gives DE_MODE_BUFDEPTH_8 = 0x000, so
92:    mode 0x10f0 (LIN_A | BUFDEPTH_8 | TYPE_YCRCB) reaches the default arm at bufdepth 1 -- ONE HEX
93:    DIGIT from the row's own MODE_YCRCB = 0x12f0.
94:  * M14: d->re_tlb_a[tlbi] is written by an ordinary guest store inside DEVICE_ACCESS
95:    (grep 'd->re_tlb_a\[tlbi\] = data\[hwi\]').  Setting bit 15 makes tileptr bit 31 set, which is
96:    what the !(tileptr & 0x80000000) check requires; a PUT with mode 0x02f0 (TLB_A | BUFDEPTH_32 |
97:    TYPE_YCRCB) then reaches the store on the tiled path.  Two guest stores.
98:
99:WHAT THEY STORE -- measured in this same table rather than asserted.  The fix DELETED the random()
100:fills, so a store re-admitted by M13/M14 writes UNASSIGNED `uint8_t buf[8]' stack bytes, not host
101:PRNG output.  M2_noguard is exactly that channel, and it reads 0x70630072 / 0x70630069 / 0x70630006
102:/ 0x70630021 across four sessions with `determinism' RED.  So an unassigned buf is guest-visible AND
103:non-deterministic, measured; M13 and M14 re-open that on the cells no row visits.
104:
105:
106:CHANNEL-LINE CHECK -- the mandate asks the first application to CHECK the line, not merely obey it
107:--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
108:PRETABLE section 4 named, per row, the mutant it kills and the observable that differs, and claimed
109:that is knowable at WRITING time before any table runs.  MEASURED: it held for ELEVEN of its twelve
110:assignments and MIS-ASSIGNED ONE.  For the single-row kills the prediction was EXACT -- M4 -> `two
111:declined', M5 -> `once', M6 -> `latch indep', M9 -> `ci8 put', M10 -> `abgr put', M12 -> `ycrcb
112:attrib'.  The one row PRETABLE declared VACUOUS BY CONSTRUCTION under a named mutant (`indep paint'
113:is fixed under M_sharedbit, and is a CONTROL for `latch indep') measured exactly so: M6 reddens
114:`latch indep' alone.  That is the honest disclosure working rather than failing.
115:
116:  *** THE MIS-ASSIGNMENT, AND MY FIRST DRAFT OF THIS PARAGRAPH SAID "HELD, 12 FOR 12" -- corrected
117:  in place. ***  Section 4 assigned M_declineall to `live silent'.  It is wrong, and PRETABLE's own
118:  appended section 6a (added ~04:3x by the pass-2 panel, codex) had already corrected it while this
119:  table was running: `ok = 0' for every type declines the RGBA accept PUT, so the destination keeps
120:  the PAINT and the row that reds is `live'; the announce lives in the default arm only, so `live
121:  silent' keeps its 0.  MY M8 MEASUREMENT IS THE INDEPENDENT CONFIRMATION -- M8_declineall reddens
122:  live, ci8 put, rgb put, abgr put, and NOT `live silent'.  Measured here, corrected there, agreeing.
123:
124:  So the honest verdict on the CHANNEL line's founding claim -- that a row's channel-under-its-mutant
125:  is knowable before any table runs -- is: MOSTLY YES, and the one error it made was caught by a
126:  READING SEAT one pass later rather than by the table.  The line is worth keeping and is not
127:  self-checking; it wants a second reader, which is exactly what it got.
128:
129:  The check's LIMIT, which this table also measures: the channel line validates ROWS THAT EXIST
130:  against MUTANTS ALREADY ENUMERATED.  It said nothing about M13/M14, and could not have -- those
131:  are generator-side gaps, and the channel line is explicitly a filter, not a demander of rows.
